---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import smallSparse from '../../generated/small-sparse.json';
import registry from '../../generated/registry.json';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

interface SchoolRow {
  school: string;
  name: string;
  supervisory_union: string | null;
  grade_span_class: string | null;
  grades_as_stated: string | null;
  municipality: string | null;
  municipality_basis: string;
  land_area_sq_mi: number | null;
  population: number | null;
  density: number | null;
  enrollment: number | null;
  small_screen_met: boolean | null;
  sparse_screen_met: boolean | null;
  enrollment_status: string;
  density_status: string;
  determination_status: string;
}

const data = smallSparse as unknown as {
  generated: string;
  parameter_set_ref: string;
  fiscal_year: number;
  nothing_is_computable: boolean;
  why: string[];
  counts: Record<string, number>;
  population_series_held: string[];
  schools: SchoolRow[];
};

const names = new Map(
  (registry.entities as Array<{ slug: string; name: string }>).map((e) => [e.slug, e.name]),
);

// Public schools only in the table. Independents and tech centres are in the
// registry because they are receiving schools in consolidation scenarios, but
// they are not candidates for these grants and listing them here would imply
// they were.
const rows = data.schools
  .filter((r) => r.municipality !== null)
  .sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));

const withoutTown = data.counts['schools'] - data.counts['with_municipality'];
---

<Base
  title="Small and sparse schools — Vermont School Budgets"
  description="The two tests for the Act 73 small and sparse school grants, and why no school in Vermont can be tested against them yet."
>
  <h1>Small and sparse schools</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Act 73 of 2025 creates money grants for schools that are small or spread out (sparse)
    <em>by necessity</em>. This page holds the maps and land facts those grants depend on. It does
    not say who qualifies, and it won't until someone reads the passed laws — the reason is right
    below, before the table instead of after it.
  </p>

  <div class="notice blocking">
    <strong>Nothing on this page says whether a school qualifies.</strong>
    <p>
      Three separate things stand between this table and an answer, and each one belongs to a
      different group:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {data.why.map((reason) => <li>{reason}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>
      The first two are on us. The third is the State's, and that is not a complaint: the Agency
      of Education can't decide who qualifies under rules that do not exist yet.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What the law asks for</h2>
  <p>
    Two tests, both simple math. A school is <strong>small</strong> if it has fewer students than
    a set cutoff. A school is <strong>sparse</strong> if the town it sits in has fewer than a set
    number of people per square mile <em>of land</em>. Both cutoffs live in
    <a href={`${base}/methodology/small-sparse/`}>the settings file</a>, and both are still blank.
  </p>
  <p>
    Passing both tests does not make a school eligible. To qualify, someone must also decide the
    school is small or sparse <em>by necessity</em> — because of where it is or how cut off it is,
    not because of a local choice. Nobody has made that decision for any school in Vermont, and
    the rules for making it are still just a proposal to the State Board, not law.
  </p>
  <p>
    One of the proposed rules matters for how to read this page: a school is not ruled out just
    for being <em>above</em> the student cutoff today, because the plan allows for places whose
    numbers dip for a while. The student test narrows the list of maybes; it is not a locked gate.
  </p>

  <h2>What is here, and what is not</h2>
  <table class="scroll-x">
    <caption>What we have for the two tests</caption>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Schools on our master list</th>
        <td>{data.counts['schools']}</td>
        <td>Every open school, academy, and career-tech center.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Matched to a town</th>
        <td>{data.counts['with_municipality']}</td>
        <td>
          Worked out from the school's map location (from AOE) against Census town borders — not
          from its mailing address. In Vermont, one post office name often covers parts of several
          towns.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">With land size</th>
        <td>{data.counts['with_land_area']}</td>
        <td>From the Census land-area file. Land only — using total area would make towns look
          less crowded than they are.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">With town population</th>
        <td>{data.counts['with_population']}</td>
        <td>
          {data.population_series_held.length === 0
            ? 'None yet: the Census population feed needs a key for town-level numbers, and this build did not have one.'
            : `Series held: ${data.population_series_held.join(', ')}.`}
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">With each school's own student count</th>
        <td>{data.counts['with_enrollment']}</td>
        <td>
          AOE reports student counts by <em>home district</em>, which is a town, not a single
          school. There is no per-school count in the public feed, and using a town's count in
          place of a school's would be a made-up number dressed up as data.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Tests finished</th>
        <td>{data.counts['screens_resolved']}</td>
        <td>
          Zero — and it stays zero while the cutoffs are unchecked, no matter what the rows above
          say.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  {withoutTown > 0 && (
    <p class="note">
      {withoutTown} schools are not in the table because we can't yet say which town they sit in:
      AOE gives no map location for them, or their location falls in a place we track only through
      a school district. Both cases are listed in
      <code>derived/school-municipality/</code> instead of being quietly dropped.
    </p>
  )}

  <h2>The map facts we do have</h2>
  <p class="note">
    The population and student columns are blank for the reasons above. The density column is what
    those numbers will be figured from, once we have a population series and a checked cutoff to
    compare it to.
  </p>

  <table class="scroll-x">
    <caption>
      {rows.length} schools where we know the town. FY{data.fiscal_year} view,
      figured against <code>{data.parameter_set_ref}</code>.
    </caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">School</th>
        <th scope="col">Town</th>
        <th scope="col">Grades</th>
        <th scope="col">Land area (sq mi)</th>
        <th scope="col">Population</th>
        <th scope="col">Density</th>
        <th scope="col">Small test</th>
        <th scope="col">Sparse test</th>
        <th scope="col">Necessity</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {rows.map((row) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{row.name}</th>
          <td>{row.municipality ? names.get(row.municipality) ?? row.municipality : '—'}</td>
          <td>{row.grades_as_stated ?? '—'}</td>
          <td>{row.land_area_sq_mi?.toFixed(1) ?? '—'}</td>
          <td>{row.population ?? '—'}</td>
          <td>{row.density?.toFixed(1) ?? '—'}</td>
          <td><span class="tag unverified">{row.enrollment_status.replace(/_/g, ' ')}</span></td>
          <td><span class="tag unverified">{row.density_status.replace(/_/g, ' ')}</span></td>
          <td><span class="tag undetermined">{row.determination_status}</span></td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What a blank means</h2>
  <p>
    There are four kinds, and the site never shows two of them the same way. See
    <a href={`${base}/methodology/#blanks`}>the how-it-works page</a> for the full list.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <span class="tag unverified">unverified</span> — a legal number has not been checked
      against the current law yet. <strong>This one is on us.</strong>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="tag missing-input">missing input</span> — the source document does not
      include it. <strong>A fact about the document.</strong>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="tag undetermined">undetermined</span> — the State has not made a decision
      that does not exist yet. <strong>Nobody's fault, for now.</strong>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="tag not-computable">not computable</span> — the question can't be answered
      from public data at all. <strong>This will never change.</strong> Four of the five necessity
      rules sit here for good, and that is a correct answer, not a task still to do.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p class="note">
    Generated {new Date(data.generated).toISOString().slice(0, 10)}.
  </p>
</Base>
